For elementary curves (circles and conics) from the gp package, computes:
- the point P of parameter U, and
- the first and second derivative vectors V1 and V2 at this point. The results, P, V1 and V2, are either:
- a {@link gp_Pnt | `gp_Pnt`} point and two {@link gp_Vec | `gp_Vec`} vectors, for a curve in 3D space, or
- a {@link gp_Pnt2d | `gp_Pnt2d`} point and two {@link gp_Vec2d | `gp_Vec2d`} vectors, for a curve in 2D space.
@param P Mutated in place; read the updated value from this argument after the call.
@param V1 Mutated in place; read the updated value from this argument after the call.
@param V2 Mutated in place; read the updated value from this argument after the call.

For elementary curves (circles, ellipses and hyperbolae) from the gp package, computes:
- the point P of parameter U, and
- the first, second and third derivative vectors V1, V2 and V3 at this point. The results, P, V1, V2 and V3, are either:
- a {@link gp_Pnt | `gp_Pnt`} point and three {@link gp_Vec | `gp_Vec`} vectors, for a curve in 3D space, or
- a {@link gp_Pnt2d | `gp_Pnt2d`} point and three {@link gp_Vec2d | `gp_Vec2d`} vectors, for a curve in 2D space.
@param P Mutated in place; read the updated value from this argument after the call.
@param V1 Mutated in place; read the updated value from this argument after the call.
@param V2 Mutated in place; read the updated value from this argument after the call.
@param V3 Mutated in place; read the updated value from this argument after the call.

In the following functions N is the order of derivation and should be greater than 0.
